<article class="bm-post">

  <header class="bm-col">
    <p class="bm-eyebrow">Physics Factory · Cadgen2 · consumer hardware</p>
    <h1>Introducing Cadgen2 <span class="bm-o">Design, Generate, and Test at Scale</span></h1>
    <p class="bm-standfirst">Cadgen2 serves as an AI-powered framework for engineering design exploration, functioning as a core module within BeyondMath’s Physics Factory. Working straight from user specifications, it constructs production-ready CAD assemblies, generates precise design variants, defeatured geometry, and automatically extracts DOE parameterizations from imported CAD models. This article explores a complete end-to-end case study: generating a tablet assembly from a text prompt, creating 50 distinct design variants, and analyzing their performance via explicit structural simulation.</p>

    <p class="bm-meta">BeyondMath · September 2026</p>
  </header>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>The constraint</h2>
    <p>Producing engineering-grade CAD remains a manual operation. Three costs dominate:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Synchronisation across a multipart assembly.</strong> A dimensional change propagates to mating parts, clearances, fastener positions and the specification documents. Each propagation path is a place where the model and the drawing can diverge.</li>
      <li><strong>Constraint compliance.</strong> Wall thicknesses, clearances, draft, minimum feature sizes and material allowables are enforced by review rather than by construction, so violations are found late.</li>
      <li><strong>No physical evaluation in the design environment.</strong> CAD systems do not report physical performance or cost during modelling. Assessing these requires exporting to a separate simulation tool, meshing, solving and interpreting. This loop is measured in days per iteration, which limits most programmes to a handful of candidate geometries.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Cadgen2 is an AI-driven CAD engine built to remove those three costs. It automates creation, modification and specification of assemblies, and connects directly to physics-based evaluation so that variants can be generated and assessed without leaving the workflow. Output is manufacturing-ready: per-part specifications, GD&amp;T records, a bill of materials and STEP geometry.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Capabilities</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automated design creation.</strong> Complete assemblies generated from a written specification, a sketch, a reference image or a natural language prompt.</li>
      <li><strong>Precision design variations.</strong> Model variants driven from natural-language instructions against a fully attributed component specification, so a change can be scoped to one dimension on one part or applied across an entire assembly.</li>
      <li><strong>Simulation defeaturing.</strong> Geometry prepared for meshing as part of generation rather than as a downstream cleanup pass, with the features that carry load retained and the features that only add elements removed.</li>
      <li><strong>Physics AI training data generation.</strong> Geometry variations generated across the design space, simulated and prepared for training Physics AI models.</li>
      <li><strong>End-to-end workflows.</strong> Integrates seamlessly with Physics Factory to transition from initial design space concepts to consolidated datasets, enabling physics AI model training and direct feedback into iterative design workflows.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Generated models are checked rather than assumed correct. Non-physical geometry, specification inconsistencies and constraint violations are detected and resolved during generation, and the checks run on the exported files rather than on the in-memory model.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="bm-rule bm-col">

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Creating a geometry</h2>
    <p class="bm-pull">"Can you create a geometry for a tablet computer, it should be defeatured for simulation, but it should contain all the major components: enclosure, screen, internal electronics, battery and connectors?"</p>
    <p>Given this prompt, Cadgen2 produced a baseline tablet assembly of 20 solid bodies, defeatured for simulation and dimensioned to engineering tolerances. The figure below shows the exploded assembly, the internal layout with measured component envelopes, a mid-width cross-section with layer thicknesses, and the individually defeatured components along the bottom row.</p>
  </section>

  <figure class="bm-wide">
    <img src="assets/fig1-baseline.webp" alt="Tablet assembly: exploded view of 20 solid bodies, annotated internal layout, mid-width cross-section, and the individual defeatured components" width="1800" height="1014">
    <figcaption>Baseline assembly: 124.6 × 174.2 × 8.10 mm, 257.0 g, 20 solid bodies. Every dimension shown is measured from the built geometry.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <p>The assembly comprises enclosure, chassis midframe, cover lens, bezel gasket, optical adhesive, display module, lightguide, pouch cell, cell pad, populated circuit board with seven packages, USB-C receptacle, two flat-flex cables and a power key. Each body is a separate solid carrying its own material card.</p>
    <p>Rather than being manually specified, dimensions are automatically derived. A unified layout model calculates all parameters and mating positions directly from the variant record, maintaining perfect synchronization across the multipart assembly as changes occur:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The assembly is closed. Enclosure floor, cell pad, cell, board standoffs, board, chassis plate, lightguide, display module, optical adhesive and cover lens sum to the internal height. Reducing overall thickness by 1 mm requires that 1 mm come out of those layers.</li>
      <li>Cell capacity is calculated from total cell volume assuming a volumetric energy density of 550 Wh/L and a nominal voltage of 3.7 V.</li>
      <li>The cover lens sits in a recess with 0.15 mm side clearance on a 1.20 mm ledge inboard of a 0.60 mm rim, so enclosure wall thickness bounds the active area for a given external width.</li>
      <li>The USB-C receptacle envelope is fixed at 8.94 × 7.25 × 3.16 mm. Below a given internal height it no longer clears the chassis plate, and generation raises rather than producing an interference.</li>
      <li>Board packages are packed with a 1.0 mm pairwise clearance, asserted on the built geometry.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>From the prompt to verified geometry took under an hour, including the constraint checks. The output is a bill of materials, per-part specifications and STEP files.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Generating variations</h2>
    <p class="bm-pull">"Can you generate 5 different variations of size, each with 5 different material thickness of the enclosure and two different materials? Also show me detailed drawings of the thickness changes"</p>
    <p>Given this prompt, Cadgen2 produced five footprint classes × five thickness levels × two enclosure material systems: <strong>50 distinct assemblies</strong>, 1,000 solid bodies, 1,050 STEP files, each with its own specification and BOM. Because the request resolves against an attributed component specification, the thickness change is applied to the named part dimension and allowed to propagate, rather than being applied to the assembly as a scale factor.</p>
  </section>

  <figure class="bm-wide">
    <img src="assets/fig2-variations.webp" alt="The 50-state variant set: five footprint classes at matched scale, five thickness levels in section, and two enclosure material systems in detail" width="1800" height="1190">
    <figcaption>Top: five footprint classes rendered at one parallel scale. Middle: the five thickness levels as mid-width sections on a shared z = 0 datum. Bottom: the two enclosure material systems at the rim, and the parameter ranges across the set.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <div class="bm-specs">
      <dl class="bm-spec"><dt>footprint</dt><dd>108.6×157.8 → 196×230 mm</dd></dl>
      <dl class="bm-spec"><dt>overall thickness</dt><dd>6.40 → 9.20 mm</dd></dl>
      <dl class="bm-spec"><dt>enclosure wall</dt><dd>0.70 → 1.50 mm</dd></dl>
      <dl class="bm-spec"><dt>enclosure floor</dt><dd>0.72 → 1.15 mm</dd></dl>
      <dl class="bm-spec"><dt>chassis plate</dt><dd>0.35 → 1.20 mm</dd></dl>
      <dl class="bm-spec"><dt>cover lens</dt><dd>0.50 → 0.70 mm</dd></dl>
      <dl class="bm-spec"><dt>cell capacity</dt><dd>749 → 4,736 mAh</dd></dl>
      <dl class="bm-spec"><dt>assembled mass</dt><dd>96 → 540 g</dd></dl>
    </div>
    <p>The secondary parameters, wall, floor, lens thickness and material, chassis plate thickness and material, board thickness, and cell capacity are assigned by independent Latin-square offsets over the three primary indices rather than tied to any one axis. If wall thickness were a function of the material system, as it is in production hardware where a die-cast magnesium shell is thinner than a moulded one, then wall thickness and modulus would be collinear across the set and their effects could not be separated. Measured on the resulting feature table, the largest absolute correlation between any two features that are not physically inseparable is <strong>0.46</strong>.</p>
    <p>Validation of the exported files verified three core criteria: each body forms a valid solid of the expected volume, no bodies overlap, and all bonded interfaces maintain the intended gap. All 50 variants passed every check.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="bm-rule bm-col">

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Assessing the variants</h2>
    <p>Variants can be assessed for design, cost or physical performance. For structural assessment the geometry is already in the required form, which is a consequence of decisions taken during generation rather than afterwards:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Defeaturing.</strong> Non-structural features were removed during model defeaturing, including threads, logos, textures, vents, solder fillets, BGA balls, and connector internals. Load-bearing geometry was retained: enclosure walls and floor, back-edge radii, six screw lands with blind bosses, stiffening ribs, board standoffs, the lens bonding ledge, port openings, and the pouch seal flange.</li>

      <li><strong>Interfaces.</strong> Coincident faces make a solver's connected-interfaces ambiguous, because a node on a shared face lies at zero distance from two candidate surfaces. Every bonded interface is therefore modelled with a 0.05 mm gap and closed by a tied contact: 19 per variant, emitted as solver cards with the geometry. Measured on the exported STEP, all 950 tie gaps come back at 0.050 mm to three decimal places.</li>
      <li><strong>Free clearances.</strong> A single surface-to-surface self-contact with a 0.02 mm minimum gap covers the clearances that close under impact: 0.25 mm cell to chassis for swell and board flexure, 0.15 mm on the lens-to-recess fit. Its minimum gap sits below the bond gap so it does not oppose the ties holding the same faces together.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each variant meshes to tetrahedra at a per-body characteristic length between 0.80 mm (the shield stamping) and 3.00 mm (the cell), and solved in <a target="_blank" href="https://openradioss.org/">OpenRadioss</a> as a 1.0 m drop onto a rigid plane. The reference build meshes to 136,905 nodes and 407,692 elements; its corner-first drop runs 80,000 cycles to 1.491 ms with normal termination, energy balance closing to 0.1%, and zero added mass.</p>
  </section>

  <figure class="bm-wide">
    <video class="bm-video" id="bm-drop-video" loop muted playsinline controls preload="metadata" poster="assets/corner-drop-poster.jpg">
      <source src="assets/corner-drop.mp4" type="video/mp4">
    </video>
    <figcaption class="bm-cap-mark"><a class="bm-cap-marklink" href="https://openradioss.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="OpenRadioss"><img src="assets/openradioss-mark.png" alt="OpenRadioss" width="66" height="44"></a><span>1.0 m corner-first drop on the baseline build, solved in OpenRadioss, coloured by von Mises stress with the ceiling at the enclosure material's 75 MPa yield strength. Peak strain energy is 1.45 J of the 2.52 J drop energy, at 0.806 ms.</span></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <p>The output per frame: contact and internal forces, von Mises, plastic strain, stress and strain tensors, is written as tensors in the format a training pipeline consumes, 3.8 GB for one 1.5 ms event.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Optimising the design</h2>
    <p>Where the objective is design selection rather than dataset generation, the same mechanism runs as a screening campaign. All 50 variants were solved on a coarser mesh of approximately 49,000 nodes, three concurrent runs on one workstation, 11 h 07 min of total solver time. Every run closed its energy balance: median error 0.3%, maximum 1.9%. Peak von Mises in the display stack spans <strong>130 to 1,781 MPa</strong> across the set, a factor of 13.7.</p>
  </section>

  <figure class="bm-wide">
    <video class="bm-video" id="bm-campaign-video" loop muted playsinline controls preload="metadata" poster="assets/design-campaign-poster.jpg">
      <source src="assets/design-campaign.mp4" type="video/mp4">
    </video>
    <figcaption>The campaign: one design becomes 50, each solved under the same 1.0 m corner drop, ranked on peak stress in the display stack. Panel values are measured per design.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <p>The ranking is not recoverable directly from individual parameter sweeps. The lowest-stress variant pairs a magnesium enclosure with a wide, short footprint: the shell absorbs 1,169 MPa while the display stack sees 130 MPa. The highest puts 1,781 MPa directly into the display module. Of the parameters swept, only overall thickness narrows the best quartile appreciably to 57% of its tried range. Cell capacity reaches 79% and chassis plate 82%; enclosure wall, floor, cover lens, board thickness and both material moduli span their full range within the best quartile. The response is driven by parameter interactions, which is the condition under which sampling and solving outperforms single-variable study.</p>
    <p>One qualification on interpretation. The material models are linear elastic, so no yielding or redistribution occurs and peak values at the impact corner are stress concentrations that scale with mesh refinement. The campaign supports relative ranking on a consistent mesh; it does not support absolute failure prediction, and the peaks should not be compared against ultimate strengths directly.</p>
    <p>A second case is covered in <a target="_blank" href="../20260919-cadgen2-f1-aero-doe/">the companion post</a>: real-time modification of imported aerodynamic surfaces on a Formula 1 car.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Fully AI-driven CAD design and optimisation</h2>
    <p>Cadgen2 is a component of the Physics Factory platform, and it addresses the early-stage constraint directly: generating the geometric variation that physics-based optimisation requires. Those variants train models built on BeyondMath's foundational Physics AI, and the trained model then allows a design to be optimised against the underlying physics rather than against a proxy.</p>
    <p>A design team exploring a released product needs a handful of controlled variants with correct specifications and a BOM. A training campaign needs several hundred decorrelated variants with solver decks attached. Both are a parameter table driven through one model, and in both the variant is recorded as a state rather than as a file and reproducible from a few hundred bytes, and recomputed identically on every rebuild.</p>
  </section>

